/arxi
/dist/
*.test

# Runtime state, not source.
#
# These are the directories arxi WRITES when it is used: run logs, trigger
# definitions, eval results, provider credentials. They are a user's data, and
# committing a developer's copy of them ships one machine's debris to every
# clone.
#
# The concrete harm was measured before this entry was added. Five run
# directories had been committed by accident, three of them carrying a
# writer.lock naming a pid from the machine that made them. A fresh clone
# inherited those locks, and the tool did exactly what it promises:
#
#   arxi: run directory runs/live-probe is already open for writing by pid 3811:
#   exactly one writer per log is required, because two writers produce
#   duplicate seq and an unfoldable log. If that writer is certainly dead,
#   remove runs/live-probe/writer.lock by hand after confirming no process is
#   running.
#
# That message is correct and unactionable at the same time: pid 3811 never
# existed on the cloner's machine, so "after confirming no process is running"
# asks them to account for a process they cannot see. The lock is doing its job
# on evidence that should never have been shipped.
#
# The list is the DefaultDir of each store rather than a guess, read from the
# code: evalstore "evals", modelstore "providers", toolstore "policies",
# trigstore "triggers", and cmd/arxi's runsDir "runs". A first draft of this
# entry listed "agents", which no store writes, and omitted "policies", which
# one does -- an ignore file that names directories nobody creates looks
# maintained while leaving a real one tracked.
#
# providers/ matters most: modelstore writes it 0600 because it holds
# credentials, and a mode that careful is wasted if the file is committed.
#
# The paths are anchored with a leading slash so they only match at the repo
# root. A test fixture named runs/ inside a package is a different thing and
# stays tracked.
/runs/
/triggers/
/evals/
/providers/
/policies/
